  <p>
    The first overnight hike is where most people overpack. You stand in the
    lounge room with a pile of gear, unsure what matters, so everything goes in.
    Then you carry 18 kg up the first hill and spend the night wondering why.
  </p>
  <p>
    This packing list is built the other way around. It starts with the few jobs
    your kit has to do, lists what covers each job, and flags the extras you can
    leave at home until you know what kind of hiking you enjoy.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>
        An overnight kit has four core jobs: shelter, sleep, carry, and food and
        water. Buy for those first.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your sleeping bag and mat work as one system. Read our guides to <a
          href="/guides/sleeping-bag-temperature-ratings/"
          >sleeping bag temperature ratings</a
        > and <a href="/guides/sleeping-mat-r-value/">mat R-values</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        A 40-55 L pack suits most compact overnight setups. Choose your pack
        last.
      </li>
      <li>
        In Australia, a first aid kit with a snake bandage, sun protection and a
        PLB belong on every overnight trip.
      </li>
      <li>
        Aim for a total pack weight of roughly 10-12 kg including food and water
        on a first trip.
      </li>
      <li>Test everything at home or on a campground night before you go.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Overnight hiking essentials: what do you need?</h2>
  <p>
    Every overnight kit has to shelter you, keep you warm while you sleep, carry
    your load, and feed and hydrate you. Everything else is either safety gear
    (non-negotiable) or comfort gear (optional). Sort your pile into those three
    groups before you buy anything.
  </p>

  <h3>1. Shelter</h3>
  <p>
    A tent or shelter that suits the expected wind, rain, insects and campsite
    surface. For a first trip, a simple freestanding two-person tent is worth a
    little extra weight: it pitches on hard platforms, gives a solo hiker room
    for their pack, and is easy to live with. Our <a
      href="/guides/2-person-hiking-tent-guide/">2 person hiking tent guide</a
    > covers weight, layout and weather in detail.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Sleep system</h3>
  <p>
    Your sleeping bag and sleeping mat work together. A warm bag cannot stop the
    ground drawing heat from below, and a warm mat cannot rescue a bag rated for
    a much milder night. Match the bag's <a
      href="/guides/sleeping-bag-temperature-ratings/">comfort rating</a
    > to the expected overnight low, then pick a mat with enough <a
      href="/guides/sleeping-mat-r-value/">R-value</a
    > for the season.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Pack</h3>
  <p>
    Buy the pack after the rest of your kit. Around 40-55 L is a common starting
    range for a compact overnight setup, but torso fit and a hip belt that
    actually carries the weight matter more than the number on the label.
    Compare a frame pack like the <a href="/gear/naturehike-rock-60-5/"
      >Naturehike Rock 60+5L</a
    > with a smaller frameless pack before you decide. A bulky budget sleeping bag
    or foam mat can push you towards the top of that range.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Food and water</h3>
  <p>
    For one night, cooking can be very simple: a small stove, one pot, a lighter
    and a spoon. Plan water sources before you leave, carry enough capacity for
    dry sections, and bring a way to treat water where it is needed.
  </p>

