  <h2>Hiking packing list: the full overnight checklist</h2>
  <p>
    Use this as a tick-off list the night before. Items in the first two groups
    come on every overnight hike.
  </p>

  <h3>Shelter and sleep</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Tent with poles, pegs and guy lines (or a tarp you know how to pitch)
    </li>
    <li>Sleeping bag or quilt suited to the expected overnight low</li>
    <li>Sleeping mat with enough insulation for the season</li>
    <li>Optional: inflatable pillow, or a stuff sack filled with clothes</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Clothing</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Walking clothes: quick-dry shirt, shorts or pants, hiking socks</li>
    <li>Waterproof rain jacket, even when rain is not forecast</li>
    <li>Warm layer: fleece or puffer jacket</li>
    <li>Camp clothes to sleep in, kept dry in a bag</li>
    <li>Beanie, and a spare pair of socks</li>
    <li>Broad-brim hat and sunglasses</li>
    <li>Footwear you have already walked in</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Food, water and cooking</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Water bottles or bladder (at least 2 L capacity for most day sections)
    </li>
    <li>Water treatment: filter, tablets or boiling</li>
    <li>Stove, fuel, pot, lighter and a long spoon</li>
    <li>Dinner, breakfast, lunch and snacks, plus a small emergency extra</li>
    <li>Rubbish bag to carry everything out</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Navigation and safety</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Map and a navigation app you know how to use, with offline maps</li>
    <li>First aid kit including a snake bandage (compression bandage)</li>
    <li>
      Personal locator beacon (PLB) where there is no reliable phone coverage
    </li>
    <li>Headlamp with spare batteries or a charged power bank</li>
    <li>Sunscreen, insect repellent and lip balm</li>
    <li>Trip intentions left with someone at home</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Toiletries and small items</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Toilet paper, hand sanitiser and a trowel (where no toilets exist)</li>
    <li>Toothbrush, small toothpaste and any medication</li>
    <li>Repair kit: tape, cord and a small knife</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to bring on a hike in Australian conditions</h2>
  <p>
    Most overseas packing lists miss the gear that Australian conditions make
    essential. Three items deserve special attention.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Sun protection.</strong> UV is strong year-round, including on cool and
    overcast days, and <a
      href="https://www.cancer.org.au/cancer-information/causes-and-prevention/sun-safety"
      rel="noopener">Cancer Council Australia</a
    > estimates that two in three Australians will be diagnosed with skin cancer by
    age 70. A broad-brim hat, sunscreen and sunglasses weigh very little and save
    a miserable second day.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A snake bandage.</strong> Carry a proper elastic compression bandage in
    your first aid kit and learn the pressure immobilisation technique before you
    need it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Emergency communication.</strong> Phone coverage disappears quickly in
    national parks. A PLB sends your position to rescue services when you have no
    signal. Some parks lend them out; check with the park office or local police.
    The <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/safety/bushwalking-safety"
      rel="noopener">NSW National Parks bushwalking safety advice</a
    > is a good checklist to read whichever state you walk in.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    The best safety item is a plan: someone at home who knows your route and
    when to raise the alarm.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>What size pack do you need for an overnight hike?</h2>
  <p>
    For one or two nights, 40-55 L covers most setups. Compact down bags and
    inflatable mats let you go smaller; budget synthetic bags and folding foam
    mats push you larger. If your gear only just fits, choose the bigger pack
    rather than strapping items to the outside, where they snag and get wet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Aim to keep a first-trip pack at around 10-12 kg including food and water.
    Heavier loads are manageable on short walks, but weight adds up on climbs
    and makes a first trip harder than it needs to be. If you are starting from
    scratch, our <a href="/#kits">beginner hiking kits</a> show how a complete setup
    comes together, and <a href="/build-my-kit/">Build My Kit</a> helps you fill the
    gaps in gear you already own.
  </p>

  <h2>Hiking gear list: what can you skip on a first overnight trip?</h2>
  <p>
    Plenty of popular gear is useful later but unnecessary now. Leave these at
    home until you find a real need:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Camp chairs and large camp lights</li>
    <li>A second cooking pot, mugs and plates (eat from the pot)</li>
    <li>Multiple changes of walking clothes</li>
    <li>Specialist gadgets: GPS watches, solar panels, coffee presses</li>
    <li>Trekking poles, unless you already use them or have knee trouble</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Borrowing is also a good option. A friend's tent or pack for one trip tells
    you more about what you want than any product listing.
  </p>

  <h2>How to plan a low-risk first overnight hike</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Choose a short route (under about 10 km each way) with an established
      campsite and an easy exit.
    </li>
    <li>Check the forecast, fire danger and park alerts the day before.</li>
    <li>Book the campsite if the park requires it.</li>
    <li>
      Pitch the tent and inflate the mat at home. A backyard night is the best
      shakedown you can do.
    </li>
    <li>Pack the night before and weigh the pack.</li>
    <li>Leave your trip intentions with someone reliable.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    A good overnight kit is short: shelter, sleep system, pack, food and water,
    then safety gear and the right layers. Get those right, test them at home,
    and pick an easy first route. You will learn more from one calm night out
    than from any gear list, including this one. When you are ready to fill the
    gaps, <a href="/build-my-kit/">Build My Kit</a> picks up from the gear you already
    own.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>What goes on an overnight hiking checklist?</summary>
      <p>
        Pack a tent, sleeping bag, sleeping mat, pack, rain jacket, warm layer,
        food, water with a treatment method, a stove, headlamp, navigation,
        first aid with a snake bandage, sun protection and an emergency
        communication device. The checklist above covers the full list.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How heavy should an overnight hiking pack be?</summary>
      <p>
        Around 10-12 kg including food and water is a comfortable target for a
        first trip. Many beginner setups sit higher, so keep the route short and
        flat until you know how the weight feels.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Do I need a PLB for an overnight hike?</summary>
      <p>
        Carry one wherever phone coverage is unreliable, which includes much of
        Australia's national park network. Some park offices and police stations
        lend PLBs for free.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How much water should I carry on an overnight hike?</summary>
      <p>
        It depends on heat, distance and water sources. Many hikers carry 2-3 L
        between sources and treat water at camp. Check with the park whether
        tanks or creeks are reliable before relying on them.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can I use a car camping sleeping bag for hiking?</summary>
      <p>
        You can, if it is warm enough and fits in your pack. Car camping bags
        are often heavy and bulky, so check the packed size and weight before
        committing to a pack size.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What is the most forgotten item on an overnight hike?</summary>
      <p>
        Commonly a lighter for the stove, a headlamp with charged batteries, or
        toilet paper and a trowel. Tick the list the night before to avoid
        finding out at camp.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
